# How a declared tool is obtained on this machine.
#
# This is the answer key for every dependency the modules declare, and the only place in
# the repository that knows about package managers. A module names a tool and says what
# breaks without it. This file says where that tool comes from. Keeping the two apart is
# what lets a module stay ignorant of Homebrew, and lets this file stay ignorant of spoons
# and scripts.
#
# One line per tool, name | origin | detail. The name matches the name column of a module's
# generated manifest exactly, since that is how the two are joined.
#
# Origins in use here.
#   brew       a Homebrew formula, detail is the formula name
#   cask       a Homebrew cask, detail is the cask name. Used when a GUI application ships
#              the command line tool we actually depend on
#   tap        a formula from a third party tap, detail is the full tap qualified name
#   xcode-clt  ships with the Xcode command line tools, detail says how those arrive
#   macos      ships with the operating system, detail says so and there is nothing to install
#   manual     installed by hand, detail says how, for anything no package manager covers
#   grant      a permission only a person can give, detail names the pane that gives it
#
# A declaration may also state its own origin, in the two trailing columns of a module's
# generated manifest, and some do. A plugin built to travel to another machine has to carry
# that answer with it and cannot rely on this file existing there at all. So the answer is
# written twice on purpose, and this file stays the complete one, the place a person reads to
# find where anything comes from. What makes the duplication safe is that the reconciler
# compares every stated origin against the line here and refuses a disagreement, so the two
# cannot drift apart unnoticed.
#
# A tool macOS already ships may still be mapped to a formula rather than to macos, when the
# version that ships is too old or too new to rely on. That is why trash is a formula even
# though this machine has an Apple one, the same reasoning the root CLAUDE.md records for jq.
#
# Two tools may share one origin line's detail, as ffmpeg and ffprobe do, because one
# formula provides both. The reconciler expects a Brewfile line for every distinct brew,
# cask, and tap detail here, and nothing else needs a Brewfile line.
#
# When a module declares a new tool, add its line here and, for the package manager
# origins, the matching Brewfile entry. src/check-dependencies.sh reports either half if
# it is missing, so this cannot quietly drift.

# Applications a module configures, which are dependencies like any other. A module cannot
# work without the program it configures, so it declares it and this is where it comes from.
hammerspoon   | cask      | hammerspoon
ghostty       | cask      | ghostty
tmux          | brew      | tmux
nvim          | brew      | neovim
lf            | brew      | lf
lazygit       | brew      | lazygit
herdr         | brew      | herdr
fut           | tap       | mikker/tap/fut

# Command line tools the configurations call.
fzf           | brew      | fzf
fd            | brew      | fd
ripgrep       | brew      | ripgrep
git           | brew      | git
bat           | brew      | bat
eza           | brew      | eza
chafa         | brew      | chafa
zoxide        | brew      | zoxide
atuin         | brew      | atuin
carapace      | brew      | carapace
trash         | brew      | trash
jq            | brew      | jq
stow          | brew      | stow
duti          | brew      | duti
ffmpeg        | brew      | ffmpeg
ffprobe       | brew      | ffmpeg
displayplacer | brew      | displayplacer
qalc          | brew      | libqalculate
ocr           | tap       | schappim/ocr/ocr
docker        | cask      | docker-desktop
hs            | cask      | hammerspoon
obsidian      | cask      | obsidian

# Packages that ship files rather than a command, loaded by path from a configuration.
tpm                      | brew | tpm
powerlevel10k            | brew | powerlevel10k
font-meslo-lg-nerd-font  | cask | font-meslo-lg-nerd-font

# Things no package manager here covers.
oh-my-zsh                | manual    | installed by src/install-ohmyzsh.sh from the upstream installer
zsh-autosuggestions      | manual    | installed by src/install-ohmyzsh-plugins.sh
fast-syntax-highlighting | manual    | installed by src/install-ohmyzsh-plugins.sh
fzf-tab                  | manual    | installed by src/install-ohmyzsh-plugins.sh
brew                     | manual    | installed by src/install-homebrew.sh from the upstream installer
swiftc                   | xcode-clt | installed by src/install-xcode-clt.sh, which raises Apple's installer and waits for it
cc                       | xcode-clt | installed by src/install-xcode-clt.sh, which raises Apple's installer and waits for it
xcode-select             | macos     | ships with macOS
caffeinate               | macos     | ships with macOS
curl                     | macos     | ships with macOS
perl                     | macos     | ships with macOS
defaults                 | macos     | ships with macOS
osascript                | macos     | ships with macOS
ps                       | macos     | ships with macOS
kill                     | macos     | ships with macOS
killall                  | macos     | ships with macOS
lsof                     | macos     | ships with macOS
ioreg                    | macos     | ships with macOS
open                     | macos     | ships with macOS
qlmanage                 | macos     | ships with macOS
scutil                   | macos     | ships with macOS
hidutil                  | macos     | ships with macOS
sips                     | macos     | ships with macOS
head                     | macos     | ships with macOS
tput                     | macos     | ships with macOS
file                     | macos     | ships with macOS
diff                     | macos     | ships with macOS
nohup                    | macos     | ships with macOS
networkQuality           | macos     | ships with macOS
script                   | macos     | ships with macOS

# Opt in extras, deliberately absent from the Brewfile.
#
# Every one of these is optional, every one is the backend of a feature rather than the
# feature itself, and a default install of this repository should not put a VPN client, a
# screenshot app or a node version manager on a machine that never asked for one. The Brewfile
# is what a fresh machine installs, so keeping these out of it is the only thing that actually
# decides that, which is why the origin here is manual rather than cask or brew. The reconciler
# requires a Brewfile line for a brew, cask or tap origin and requires nothing for a manual
# one, so this is the supported way to say wanted but not by default rather than a way around
# the rule.
#
# Nothing is uninstalled by moving a line here. The declarations stay, the plugins still work
# on a machine that has the tool, and the reconciler still reports an absent one as a warning
# so the answer to why is this row missing is one command away rather than a mystery.
#
# The detail names the package rather than a command to run, which is the same line the rest of
# this file walks, so a person knows what to install without this file turning into a script.
fnm           | manual    | the fnm formula, for node version switching. .zshrc.custom guards its hook, so an absent fnm costs a shell nothing
mullvad       | manual    | the mullvad-vpn cask, wanted only on a machine that uses Mullvad. Without it the VPN picker simply has one fewer backend
macshot       | manual    | the macshot cask, the preferred screenshot backend. The capture plugin falls back to the native one without it
# ivpn is the one entry here where installing is not the whole answer, so this is worth
# reading before installing it. The cask ships only the App artifact, copied out of a DMG, and
# that copy normalizes three files inside the bundle to 644. IVPN's daemon refuses to start
# unless they are unreadable by group and other, and IVPN's own pkg installer is what would
# have set them to 600. A dead daemon also means no ivpn command, since the agent is what
# symlinks it onto the PATH. src/setup-ivpn-permissions.sh restores the modes, setup.sh runs
# it on every run, and it exits cleanly on a machine that does not have IVPN at all.
ivpn          | manual    | the ivpn cask, wanted only on a machine that uses IVPN, and then src/setup-ivpn-permissions.sh to make its daemon able to start

# Gates rather than tools. Nothing here can be installed, and no script can open one. A module
# declares a gate the way it declares a tool, the reconciler proves it the way it proves a tool,
# and these lines say what a person has to do. Which is the whole point of moving them here,
# since a gate that lives only in prose is one you find by watching a feature fail quietly.
accessibility            | grant     | System Settings, Privacy and Security, Accessibility, turn Hammerspoon on
obsidian-vaults          | manual    | open a vault in Obsidian once, which is what writes the registry this plugin reads

# Tools a module's own tooling needs rather than its running configuration. A generated
# manifest is built by reading the declarations inside the module, and the Hammerspoon module
# writes its declarations in Lua, so reading them needs Lua. That is the module's own business
# and it declares the tool like any other rather than assuming it.
lua                      | brew      | lua
